# ─── ROLE CONTRACT (break-glass / RLS depends on this — design §10) ──────────
# The two-role split below is LOAD-BEARING for both tenant RLS and break-glass:
#   DATABASE_URL            MUST be the `campass_app` role (NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS)
#                           over the TRANSACTION pooler (6543). It backs BOTH the
#                           runtime tenant client (`withTenant`) AND the break-glass
#                           client (`breakGlassPrisma` / `withSupportGrant`), so the
#                           tenant GUC is a REAL enforcing layer for a break-glass read.
#   PRIVILEGED_DATABASE_URL MUST be the `postgres` role (BYPASSRLS). DIRECT_URL is the
#   / DIRECT_URL            postgres-role fallback for migrations/DDL and the privileged
#                           client.
# WARNING: pointing DATABASE_URL at the postgres (BYPASSRLS) role silently defeats BOTH
# tenant RLS AND break-glass scoping. The startup NOBYPASSRLS probe in
# `withSupportGrant` turns that misconfiguration into a HARD FAILURE (fail-closed),
# not a silent cross-tenant leak. No NEW secret is introduced by FEAT-003.
#
# ─── Database (Supabase Postgres) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE_URL  Pooled (Supavisor transaction mode, port 6543) — app runtime.
#               MUST include ?pgbouncer=true (transaction mode has no prepared statements).
# DIRECT_URL    Migrations / DDL. Direct host (db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432) is IPv6-only;
#               if unreachable, use the Session pooler (pooler host, port 5432, IPv4).
# Get both from: Supabase Dashboard → Connect.
# DATABASE_URL  App runtime (tenant-scoped). The `campass_app` role (NOSUPERUSER
#               NOBYPASSRLS) over the TRANSACTION pooler (6543) so RLS enforces. The
#               ?pgbouncer=true flag is REQUIRED (transaction mode, no prepared stmts).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://campass_app.<project-ref>:<campass_app-password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"
# DIRECT_URL    Migrations / DDL. The postgres role over the Session pooler (5432).
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<postgres-password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"
# PRIVILEGED_DATABASE_URL  Used by the `privilegedPrisma` client to BYPASS Row-Level
#               Security for pre-auth/platform routes (login, register, /departments,
#               the authenticate middleware, and SUPER_ADMIN platform routes). It MUST
#               point at the postgres/owner role (RLS-bypass), NEVER at `campass_app`.
#               Use the postgres role over the TRANSACTION pooler (6543, ?pgbouncer=true):
#               the postgres role bypasses RLS regardless of port, and 6543 is reliable
#               whereas the 5432 session pooler can be slow/unreachable on some networks.
#               The privileged client issues only simple reads + a short $transaction
#               (no session-level features), so transaction-mode pooling is safe.
#               If left unset, the app falls back to DIRECT_URL, then DATABASE_URL.
#               Set the real value LOCALLY only; do NOT commit it.
PRIVILEGED_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<postgres-password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"

# ─── API ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
JWT_SECRET="change-this-to-a-secure-random-string"
FRONTEND_URL="http://localhost:3000"
PORT=4000
HOST=0.0.0.0
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ─── Razorpay (Phase 7 fees — INR) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# All fee amounts are INTEGER PAISE (e.g. 5000000 = ₹50,000); Razorpay's `amount`
# field is already in paise, so there is no unit conversion on the boundary.
# REAL keys are NEVER committed and `.env` is NEVER staged — these are placeholders.
# Use TEST-mode keys for local/dev; LIVE-mode keys only in production.
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_ID        The publishable key id — the ONLY Razorpay value ever sent
#                          to a client (returned with the order so checkout can open).
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET    The API secret — NEVER leaves the server. Used to create
#                          orders and to verify the checkout payment signature.
#   RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET The webhook signing secret — NEVER leaves the server. Used
#                          to verify the X-Razorpay-Signature HMAC over the raw body.
# In production the app fails hard at startup if KEY_SECRET / WEBHOOK_SECRET are unset
# (mirrors the JWT_SECRET guard); in dev/test a mock/absent secret must not crash.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID="rzp_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET="your-razorpay-key-secret"
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET="your-razorpay-webhook-secret"
#
# OWNER-ROLE REQUIREMENT FOR THE WEBHOOK (design §D.8):
# PRIVILEGED_DATABASE_URL (or, as its fallback, DIRECT_URL) MUST point at the
# Postgres owner role (BYPASSRLS), NEVER at `campass_app`. The Razorpay webhook has
# no JWT and no tenant context, so its pre-tenant order lookup (resolve the tenant
# from the stored PaymentTransaction by razorpayOrderId) REQUIRES a true
# RLS-bypassing connection. If that lookup resolves to an RLS-subject role it returns
# zero rows and every real captured payment would be silently 200-acked into the void;
# a fail-closed role probe therefore throws loudly instead. (No NEW secret is added —
# PRIVILEGED_DATABASE_URL/DIRECT_URL are already configured above.)
